## DISCRIMINATING TEST -- SUPERSEDED BELOW, kept because the error is the point
 for the -14.1% marginal-term drop (designed BEFORE running it)
Question: is the saving on the GATE PATH (per AND-message word) or in PER-RESPONSE FIXED COSTS
(PRG construction, hasher setup)?
Method: refit the hardened-child law with cycle-probe (execute-only) and compare the per-AND-word
saving against the elephant's. The two statements are ~50x apart in AND-message words:
  ethereum address 368,123   hardened child 7,417
Elephant saving: (2.4627e9 - 2.1148e9) / 368,123 = 945 instructions per AND-message word.
PREDICTIONS, written down first:
  - GATE PATH  => hardened-child marginal falls by ~945 x 7,417 = 7.01e6, i.e. 21.66e6 -> ~14.65e6
                  (a ~32% drop), and per-AND-word savings agree between the two statements.
  - FIXED COST => hardened-child marginal falls by roughly the same ABSOLUTE amount per response
                  as the elephant's does, which would be a tiny fraction of 0.3479e9, and the
                  per-AND-word figures differ by ~50x.
Crude prior from the n=219 totals alone (4.7750e9 -> 4.3867e9), IF the fixed term is unchanged:
  0.3883e9 / 219 = 1.773e6 per response => 239 instr/AND-word, ~4x below the elephant's 945.
  That points at FIXED COST, but it ASSUMES an unmoved fixed term, which is exactly the thing
  that has to be measured rather than assumed. Do not quote it; run the refit.
Must run on a quiet machine, after split-probe finishes -- concurrent runs contaminate both.

## *** THE TEST ABOVE USED THE WRONG DENOMINATOR, AND A NUMBER FROM THE WRONG FILE ***
Two errors, both mine, caught by the zkboo session.

ERROR A -- provenance. I used 7,417 AND-messages for the hardened child. That figure is from
  <zkboo>/succinct-lifter/knowledge/cost-numbers.md -- the table I had MYSELF flagged, in
  writing, as sitting under a withdrawn-version header and as not this repo's to edit. My own
  knowledge/elephant-cost.md:52 records 6,454, on the same line that states the 57x ratio.
  Identifying a source as untrustworthy and then quoting it anyway is worse than not checking.
  Correct figure, from this repo: hardened child 6,454, ethereum address 368,123, ratio 57.0x.

ERROR B -- normaliser. AND-messages are the wrong quantity to divide by. The zkboo session
  profiled TOTAL backend operations:
      ethereum address  and_msgs 368,123   total_ops 11,964,609   (32:1 -- field arithmetic is
      hardened child    and_msgs   6,454   total_ops     45,651    overwhelmingly LINEAR)
      ratio by AND-messages 57.0x   ratio by total ops 262.1x
  (their caveat: total_ops excludes `cast`, and counts a `not` the same as a `bitand`.)

WHAT THAT DOES TO MY READING: it reverses it. I wrote that the crude figure "leans FIXED COST".
  It refutes fixed cost outright, and by arithmetic alone, needing no run:
  a per-response fixed saving is the SAME ABSOLUTE amount for both statements, so the hardened
  child would have to save 0.3479e9 instructions per response -- more than sixteen times its
  ENTIRE marginal term of 21.66e6. Its marginal would go negative. Impossible.
  Observed saving ratio 0.3479e9 / 1.773e6 = 196x, against predictions: fixed 1x (off 196x),
  per-AND-word 57x (off 3.4x), per-operation 262x (off 1.34x). Per-operation wins.
  This SHARPENS the recorders hypothesis: the SSA wire-id bus assigned an id on every write to a
  storage bit and looked one up on every read -- per OPERATION, not per AND gate, which is why
  the AND-normalised numbers looked incoherent.

REVISED PREDICTIONS, written before the run, as the NEW hardened-child marginal term:
  fixed-cost model     REFUTED by arithmetic above; no run needed.
  per-AND-word (57.0x) delta 0.3479e9/57.0  = 6.10e6  => new marginal ~15.56e6
  per-operation (262x) delta 0.3479e9/262.1 = 1.33e6  => new marginal ~20.33e6
  crude "fixed term unchanged" back-of-envelope from the n=219 totals: delta 1.773e6 => ~19.89e6
    (this ASSUMES an unmoved fixed term; the refit exists to measure it, not assume it)
  The two live models are ~4.8e6 apart in the marginal term, so the refit separates them cleanly.
  Old law for reference: 3.147e7 + 21.66e6 x n (derived from the recorded 4.775013465e9 at n=219).

LESSON, and it is not the same as the one I recorded an hour ago: it was the NORMALISER that was
  wrong first, not the mechanism. A ratio test is only as good as the quantity you divide by, and
  here the obvious quantity -- the one the whole cost model is built around -- was not the
  operative one. Keep this next to "refit, never scale".

