## PREDICTIONS for elephant-split 2 both, written BEFORE the run (2026-09-07)
First, TWO WRONG MODELS, both caught before the run:
  MINE: I said "leaf ~4.6% cheaper", got by evaluating the MONOLITHIC law 5.0986e9+2.1148e9n at
    n=1 against 5.1004e9+2.4627e9n. Wrong: a LEAF PAYS NO FIXED TERM. program-leaf-elephant
    deliberately computes no circuit fingerprint -- that is its whole design point, since a
    per-leaf traversal would cost ~2 extra responses each. So the fixed term must not be in a
    leaf estimate at all.
  THE ZKBOO SESSION'S: they evaluated at n=24 and n=110 ("219 over two leaves"). Wrong the other
    way: `elephant-split n` proves n leaves of ONE response each (responses[position], 0..n), so
    a leaf is n=1 and `elephant-split 2` is 2 leaves x 1 response, not 2 leaves x 110.
  Neither model was checked against the driver until now. The driver settles it.

PREDICTIONS:
  LEAF instructions: the previous round measured leaf = 0.973 x the monolithic marginal term.
    old 0.973 x 2.4627e9 = 2.396e9  ->  new 0.973 x 2.1148e9 = 2.058e9, i.e. -14.1%
    ...but LESS than 14.1% in practice, because a leaf also parses its frame and encodes a
    journal, work that is not zkboo replay and did not get cheaper. Expect 12-14%.
  ROOT: expect ESSENTIALLY UNCHANGED. The root's cost is the circuit-fingerprint traversal (the
    fixed term, which held to -0.035%) plus SP1 recursion (not zkboo code at all) plus the
    cross-leaf challenge re-derivation (negligible). None of that is touched by the -14.1%,
    which is per-response REPLAY and lives in the leaves.
  WALL CLOCK OVERALL: the root was 79% of the split's critical path, so an improvement confined
    to the leaves should move the total by roughly 0.21 x 13% = ~3%. If the total moves much
    more than that, something other than the replay path got cheaper and I have mis-modelled it.
  A cheap discriminator exists and will be run FIRST: `elephant-split 2 execute` measures one
  leaf's instruction count directly and prints its ratio to the marginal term. That converts the
  leaf prediction into a measurement before ~6 hours of proving, rather than after.

## PREDICTION CORRECTED AGAIN, before the run -- my hedge pointed the wrong way
I wrote "expect 12-14%, LESS than 14.1%, because frame parsing and journal encoding did not get
cheaper". Directionally wrong, and by my own rule: I reapplied the 0.973 RATIO -- measured under
the old code -- to the NEW marginal term. That is scaling a thing whose parts moved separately.
Decompose instead. `other` (frame parse, journal encode) is a CONSTANT, not a fraction:
  leaf_old = replay_old + other = 2,395,132,641   (MEASURED, fixtures/elephant-execute-gate...)
  leaf_new = leaf_old - (2.4627e9 - 2.1148e9) = 2,047,232,641  =>  -14.52%
A constant `other` makes the BASE smaller while the absolute saving is unchanged, which pushes
the percentage UP, not down: 0.3479e9 on 2.396e9 is 14.5%, on 2.4627e9 it is 14.1%.
REVISED BAND: 13-15%, central 14.5%. A measured 14.5% would have fallen OUTSIDE my old band and
read as a surprise when it is the central case.
Two assumptions remain, and the execute run settles both: that a leaf replays one response
identically to the monolith's per-response increment, and that the whole 0.3479e9 is replay
rather than partly per-response loop overhead a leaf never had. If a slice is loop overhead, the
leaf saving is smaller -- and then 12-14% is right for a reason I had not given.

## STALE-MEASUREMENT AUDIT (2026-09-07) -- five instances of one pattern
The tell: is a constant a DEFINITION (safe to embed) or a MEASUREMENT (a claim nothing
re-checks)? Grepping for the latter found, in this repo:
  elephant_split.rs `2.4627e9` hardcoded as "the marginal" -- silently became the OLD marginal,
    in the very instrument meant to settle a before-and-after question. Fixed earlier today.
  elephant_probe.rs HASH_MARGINAL_INSTR / HASH_FULL_INSTRUCTIONS / HASH_FULL_SECONDS -- three
    2.0.0-era measurements driving a live days-at-full-scale headline. TWO of the replacements
    I had measured MYSELF hours earlier without noticing they were also in a tool.
Plus, outside this repo: zkboo's PROOF_FORMAT_VERSION, and the withdrawn-version cost table that
leaked 7,417 into a live prediction of mine this afternoon.

THE CURE IS PLACEMENT OF THE CHECK, not avoidance of the constant. zkboo's PROOF_FORMAT_ID is a
measurement, in src, exported -- and safe only because a test recomputes it every run. Remedies
in order of strength: (1) re-derive at test time and FAIL on drift; (2) do not embed at all, have
the instrument measure its own baseline; (3) provenance only -- the fallback, which tells a
reader when to be suspicious and never when to be correct.

APPLIED to elephant_probe.rs:
  HASH_MARGINAL_INSTR    -> DELETED. hardened_child_law() executes the guest at n=1,2,4 and
                            refits on every run (~2 s inside a driver that runs for minutes).
  HASH_FULL_INSTRUCTIONS -> DELETED. Derived from that law at n=219.
  HASH_FULL_SECONDS      -> kept, and marked IN THOSE WORDS as the only one that can carry
                            provenance alone, because re-deriving it means re-running lift 219.
  THREE points, not two: two determine the law exactly and leave NO residual, so a two-point fit
  cannot report its own model breaking. The residual is ASSERTED at 0.05% (the five-point fit
  came in under 0.0032%), not printed -- a threshold that only warns is annotation again.
  🪤 HASH_FULL_SECONDS is currently the undefined identifier PENDING_LIFT_219, so the crate does
  NOT BUILD until the measured value replaces it. A placeholder is this disease at its purest -- a
  measurement-shaped constant with no measurement behind it -- so it is made impossible to ship.

