================================================================================
🔴 CORRECTION — the on-chain gas figures were measuring the TEST HARNESS.
   This retracts the attribution recorded above AND corrects a headline number
   this project has been publishing since 2026-08-28.
================================================================================
An hour ago I recorded the pairing check at 272,635 gas (was 270,441) and wrote
"⚠️ Neither gas delta is attributed... both correlate with the journal growing 31 bytes".
Correlation is not attribution, so I tested it, and the story is wrong in an
interesting way: the bytes ARE the cause, but not through the verifier.

METHOD.  Both fixtures are internally consistent and carry their own verifying key, so
the SAME deployed verifier accepts both and does the same pairing work on each.  The only
variable is where the bytes live.  One call per test function — two calls in one test are
not comparable, because the second finds everything warm.  🪤 My FIRST attempt did exactly
that and reported the 219-byte journal as CHEAPER than the 188-byte one.  That is not a
result, it is a warm-access artefact, and it is the same class of mistake as the ones
already recorded above.

  `SP1VerifierGroth16.verifyProof`      188-byte journal   219-byte journal   delta
    values memory-resident                     225,838            225,917       +79
    values in warm storage                     228,388            228,576      +188
    values in COLD storage                     270,441            272,635    +2,194
                                               ^^^^^^^            ^^^^^^^
    ← both reproduced TO THE GAS, which is what identifies the arrangement the suite
      was using.  The published 270,441 is the cold-storage form.

  `LiftJournalGate.check`                (the old gate cannot decode a 188-byte journal —
    values memory-resident                      54,610       it refuses with JournalTruncated,
    values in COLD storage                      89,322       correctly, so no cross-version row)

WHAT WAS ACTUALLY HAPPENING.  The test contract holds the fixture in `bytes internal`
fields.  Forge resets the EVM access list per test, so reading them inside the measured
region charges a COLD SLOAD (2,100) per 32-byte word — journal plus proof, about 46,700
gas on the pairing path and about 34,700 on the gate.  A relying contract receives both
in calldata and pays none of it.

So the +2,194 is one extra cold storage slot: the journal crossed from six 32-byte words
to seven (188 → 219 bytes).  Byte length was the cause; the mechanism was the test's own
storage layout, not the verifier.  On memory-resident values those 31 bytes cost 79 gas.

CORRECTED HEADLINE, for what a relying contract actually pays in the call:
    pairing check   225,975
    journal gate     54,615
    together        280,590        (was published as ~356,000, then ~361,960)
The published figures overstate by ~81,000 gas, about 23%.
⚠️ The transaction ALSO pays intrinsic calldata for 219 + 356 = 575 bytes, roughly 9,000
gas at 16/byte, plus the 21,000 base.  That is outside the call and outside both figures.

FIXED, not just noted.  Both `test_gas` functions now copy to memory before starting the
clock, so the instruments report what a caller pays.  The storage figure is recorded in
their doc comments with its provenance rather than re-measured in-suite — because a
second call in the same test is warm, which is the same trap one level down.

🔑 The general shape, and it is the third instance today: an instrument that has to hold
its own fixture will charge the subject for holding it.  Ask where the data lives before
believing what the meter says.

