  measured under the unfixed build, 64 proofs predict 2.29 failures and seeing none has
  probability **10.1%**.  So this is evidence at about the ten-percent level — NOT a
  demonstration.  Reaching 5% would need ~84 proofs and 1% ~129.  Upstream's own 400-proof
  soak is far stronger, but on a different workload; this one is on the workload the rate was
  measured on, which is what they asked for and what neither of us had.

  🔑 Run 1 (REUSE off) does NOT add to this power.  With the hand-off disabled the fault has
  no supply, so a clean run there is not evidence about the repair — only about the default.
  The two runs answer two different questions and must not be pooled.

  Cost of the hand-off, incidentally: 58.66 s/leaf with it against 59.53 s without, both under
  check mode.  Within run-to-run scatter, which agrees with upstream's measurement that it
  pays nothing — the reason it now defaults off.

################################################################################
ITEM 3 — peak RSS, like for like.  The confound MATTERED: the two effects OPPOSE.
################################################################################
Elephant leaves-only, n=2, one process, on the SAFE path — matching the configuration the
2.0.0 baseline was taken on, so only the release differs.
  leaf 0  3,876.50 s   leaf 1  3,864.78 s   mean 3,870.64 s
  7,749.57 real, peak RSS **81.71 GB**
  fixtures/elephant-leaves-safegpu-2026-09-08.summary.log

  LEAF PHASE, peak RSS:
    2.0.0, safe   76.05 GB
    1.2.0, safe   81.71 GB     ← release effect       **+7.43%**
    1.2.0, full   57.40 GB     ← configuration effect **−29.75%**

  🔑 **The release made peak memory WORSE by 7.4%, and the backend configuration more than
  paid for it.**  The naive comparison — 2.0.0-safe against 1.2.0-full — reads −24.5% and
  attributes to the release an improvement that is entirely the backend's, over the top of a
  regression going the other way.  That is exactly the confound the "not measured" entry
  refused to guess at yesterday, and guessing either way would have been wrong.

  🪤 Note it would have been wrong in the *flattering* direction.  A confounded comparison
  that happens to favour your own change is the one you are least likely to interrogate.

  Timing cross-check, same configuration: 3,870.64 s leaves-only today against 3,919.77 s in
  yesterday's leaves+root process — **1.25% apart**, which is a useful run-to-run noise floor
  for this workload and confirms the two runs are measuring the same thing.

ROOT PHASE, safe path, n=2 — the second half of the isolation.
  root 15,248.26 s, gate green, peak RSS **80.17 GB**
  fixtures/elephant-root-safegpu-2026-09-08.summary.log

  ROOT PHASE, peak RSS:
    2.0.0, safe   74.37 GB   (a rerun of the same gave 74.70, spread +0.43% — the noise floor)
    1.2.0, safe   80.17 GB   ← release effect       **+7.79%**
    1.2.0, full   53.03 GB   ← configuration effect **−33.85%**
    naive (2.0.0-safe vs 1.2.0-full): −28.69%, confounded

  🔑 **Both phases agree: +7.43% on the leaf, +7.79% on the root.**  Two workloads that share
  almost nothing — a view replay against a one-off curve fingerprint traversal — moved by the
  same amount to within four tenths of a percent, against a baseline whose own run-to-run
  spread is 0.43%.  That is a systematic release effect, not scatter, and it is the one thing
  in this whole comparison that a single phase could not have established.

  ROOT PHASE, seconds, like for like on the safe path:
    2.0.0  15,589.86 / 15,493.61 (two runs)   1.2.0  15,248.26
    −2.19% / −1.58%, against 1.2.0's own run-to-run of −1.47% (15,476.43 yesterday).
    So the release's effect on root TIME is inside the scatter — the "root unchanged" reading
    recorded yesterday holds, now with a measured noise floor under it rather than an assertion.

