  **zkboo 1.2.0 costs about 7.5% more peak memory on both phases.**  It was invisible until
  now because the only comparison available crossed the backend change as well, and that
  change is four times larger and points the other way.  The naive figures (−24.5%, −28.7%)
  would have credited the release with a memory improvement it did not make.

  🪤 And they would have flattered us.  A confounded comparison that favours your own change
  is the one least likely to be interrogated — which is the argument for having written "not
  measured" rather than a number, two days ago, when only the confounded pair existed.

################################################################################
2026-09-09 — WORKSPACE RESTRUCTURE, and what it cost.
################################################################################
Crates moved into `crates/` (host-side) and `programs/` (zkVM guests), and renamed
`sp1-lifter-*` to `pofseed-sp1-lift-*`.  crates.io has no namespace mechanism and all eleven
crates are `publish = false`, so the prefix is for local readability only.

MEASURED FIRST, on the monolithic lift guest, each change independently:
    baseline                                          lift vkey 0x00f1810f…
    package renamed only                                        0x00b1555b…
    folder moved only, package name intact                      0x00c556ba…
Both are sufficient on their own and they give different keys, so neither subsumes the other:
the guest ELF carries its package name AND its source paths.

Circuit fingerprints did NOT move (bd121f6f… / a64f0f8a…), nor did the proof-format id.
So the restructure invalidates exactly the key-pinned artefacts and nothing else.

TRAP, confirmed rather than reasoned about: immediately after the rename `forge test` went
GREEN.  The fixture and its pin were both stale and therefore agreed with each other, so the
suite attested a program that no longer existed.  Only `vkeys` catches this.  The README now
says to run it after moving or renaming a crate, not only after editing one.

WHAT IT COST
  re-pin `pinned-keys`: LEAF_VK, LEAF_ELEPHANT_VK — converged in one pass
  regenerate the on-chain fixture: `lift 219 groth16`
  re-pin EXPECTED_VKEY → 0x00883344bf73a83ef3c2122a542e1c9d01f06d7505ac8cbae77a3837a4bf0c6c
  discard 142 MB of cached leaf proofs, all unverifiable under the new keys, and re-prove them

A FIGURE THAT CAME FREE WITH THE REGENERATION — the first full-soundness lift on the repaired
backend:
    safe path   9,927.49 s / 76.52 GB
    full path   3,613.03 s / 48.39 GB      **2.748x faster, 63% of the memory**
  The headline lift is 1 h 00 m 13 s, not 2 h 45 m.
  A fourth workload, a fourth distinct speedup: 2.52x (child leaf), 2.62x (elephant root),
  2.748x (monolithic lift), 2.94x (elephant leaf).  The spread is now 17%.

ACCEPTANCE AFTER THE RESTRUCTURE
  vkeys PASS (both pins correct)          forge test 18/18
  s1-acceptance: reject matrix PASS, full-scale positive PASS (4,386,739,693 instructions)
  cross-leaf gate matrix PASS ×4; forge-vk, forge-count, forge-order all refuse
  child leaves re-proved (22.81 s mean); elephant leaves re-proving
